##### **1.6 Pursuit Dynamics: Symmetric Systems and Straight-Line Tracking**

**Collinear pursuit** defines motion where a pursuer tracks a target moving along the same rectilinear trajectory. When a target moves with constant speed $v_A$ and a pursuer with constant speed $v_C > v_A$ begins at an initial distance $S$ behind the target, the **relative closing speed** is:

$$\Delta v = v_C - v_A$$

The capture time required for the separation to vanish is:

$$t = \frac{S}{v_C - v_A}$$

For example, if an antelope runs at $v_A = 88\text{ km/h}$ with an initial head start of $S = 40\text{ m}$ and a cheetah pursues at $v_C = 101\text{ km/h}$, the relative closing speed is $\Delta v = 13\text{ km/h} \approx 3.61\text{ m/s}$, yielding a capture duration of $t \approx 11.1\text{ s}$.

In multi-dimensional kinematics, **pursuit along the line of sight** requires the pursuer's velocity vector $\vec{v}_P$ to remain directed at the target's instantaneous position $\vec{r}_T$. Let $\vec{r} = \vec{r}_T - \vec{r}_P$ denote the relative separation vector of magnitude $l(t) = |\vec{r}|$, and let $\hat{u} = \vec{r}/l$ denote the unit vector pointing along the instantaneous line of sight from pursuer to target. The scalar **closing rate** $-\frac{dl}{dt}$ equals the net rate of distance reduction evaluated by projecting both velocity vectors onto the line of sight:

$$-\frac{dl}{dt} = (\vec{v}_P - \vec{v}_T) \cdot \hat{u} = v_{P,\parallel} - v_{T,\parallel}$$

where $v_{P,\parallel} = \vec{v}_P \cdot \hat{u}$ is the component of the pursuer's velocity directed toward the target, and $v_{T,\parallel} = \vec{v}_T \cdot \hat{u}$ is the target's velocity component along the same orientation.

